(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-02"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Solids keep their shape. Liquids and gases do not: they take the shape of their container and they flow when a force acts. This chapter studies fluids at rest (pressure, surface tension) and fluids in motion (viscosity, continuity, Bernoulli)."},"2.2":{"s":"A fluid is anything that can flow: liquids, gases and plasma. A fluid cannot resist a shearing (sideways) force for long; it keeps deforming. That is the key difference from a solid."},"2.3":{"s":"Pressure is the normal force per unit area, p = F/A, in pascal. In a liquid, pressure at depth h is hρg more than at the surface. It does not depend on the shape of the vessel (the hydrostatic paradox). Gauge pressure = absolute pressure minus atmospheric pressure. Pascal's law: pressure applied to an enclosed fluid reaches every part equally. This is how a hydraulic lift multiplies force. Manometers and barometers measure pressure.","f":[{"x":"p = F/A","n":"Pressure: normal force per unit area; unit Pa = N/m^2. 1 bar = 10^5 Pa.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.27"},{"x":"p = h*rho*g","n":"Pressure due to a liquid column of height h and density rho.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.28"},{"x":"p = p_0 + h*rho*g","n":"Absolute pressure at depth h; p_0 = atmospheric pressure. Gauge pressure = p - p_0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.29"},{"x":"F_2 = F_1*(A_2/A_1)","n":"Hydraulic lift (Pascal's law): small force F_1 on the small piston A_1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.3, p.31"}],"w":["Pressure at a depth depends only on the depth, not on the vessel's shape or width.","Pressure is a scalar: at a point it acts equally in all directions."]},"2.4":{"s":"A liquid surface acts like a stretched skin. Surface tension is force per unit length in the surface (N/m), equal to surface energy per unit area. Surface molecules are pulled inward, so drops are round. The angle of contact decides whether a liquid wets glass. Impurities and higher temperature usually lower surface tension. A curved surface has extra pressure inside (2T/R for a drop). Liquids rise in thin tubes: h = 2T cos θ / (rρg).","f":[{"x":"T = F/L","n":"Surface tension: force per unit length along a line in the surface (N/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.35"},{"x":"W = T*dA","n":"Work to increase the surface area by dA = surface energy; so T is also energy per unit area.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.36"},{"x":"p_i - p_0 = 2*T/r","n":"Excess pressure inside a drop of radius r.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.41"},{"x":"p_i - p_0 = 4*T/r","n":"Soap bubble: two surfaces, so twice the drop value.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.41"},{"x":"h = 2*T*cos(theta)/(r*rho*g)","n":"Capillary rise in a tube of radius r; theta = angle of contact. Negative h (a fall) when theta is more than 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.4, p.43"}],"w":["A soap film or bubble has TWO surfaces: double the area (and force) in every calculation.","Mercury in glass has an obtuse angle of contact, so it falls in a capillary and forms a convex meniscus."]},"2.5":{"s":"Moving fluids. In streamline flow every particle follows a smooth path and paths never cross. In turbulent flow the motion is irregular and swirling. A real fluid also has internal friction between its layers, called viscosity.","w":["Two streamlines can never cross; that would give two velocities at one point."]},"2.6":{"s":"Above a certain speed, called the critical velocity, streamline flow turns turbulent. The Reynolds number, ρvd/η, has no unit and tells which kind of flow to expect: small values mean streamline flow, large values mean turbulent flow. Viscosity is measured by the coefficient of viscosity η, the stress needed per unit velocity gradient.","f":[{"x":"v_c = R_n*eta/(rho*d)","n":"Critical velocity; R_n = Reynolds number, eta = coefficient of viscosity, d = tube diameter.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.45"},{"x":"R_n = v*rho*d/eta","n":"Reynolds number (no unit): below about 1000 streamline, above about 2000 turbulent.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.45"},{"x":"f = eta*A*(dv/dx)","n":"Newton's law of viscosity; A = area of the layer, dv/dx = velocity gradient. SI unit of eta: N s/m^2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.6, p.46"}],"w":["A here is the area of the sliding layer, not the cross-section of the pipe.","Heating lowers the viscosity of a liquid but raises that of a gas."]},"2.7":{"s":"Stokes' law: a small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid feels a drag force F = 6πηrv. A falling sphere speeds up until drag plus buoyancy balance its weight. After that it falls at a steady terminal velocity, which is proportional to r².","f":[{"x":"F = 6*pi*eta*r*v","n":"Stokes' law: viscous drag on a small sphere of radius r moving at speed v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.7, p.47"},{"x":"v = 2*r^2*(rho - sigma)*g/(9*eta)","n":"Terminal velocity; rho = density of the sphere, sigma = density of the fluid.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.7, p.48"}],"w":["If the fluid is denser than the sphere (σ > ρ), the terminal velocity is upward: a bubble rises."]},"2.8":{"s":"For steady flow of a liquid that cannot be compressed, the volume passing any cross-section per second is the same: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. So a liquid flows faster where the pipe or river is narrow, and slower where it is wide.","f":[{"x":"A_1*v_1 = A_2*v_2","n":"Equation of continuity: volume flow rate A v is the same at every section (incompressible, steady flow).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.8, p.49"}],"w":["Speed goes as 1/area, so as 1/(diameter)²: half the diameter means four times the speed."]},"2.9":{"s":"Bernoulli's equation is conservation of energy for a flowing ideal fluid: p + ½ρv² + ρgh is constant along a streamline. Where the fluid moves faster, its pressure is lower. This explains aircraft wing lift, the Venturi meter, sprayers and the speed of liquid coming out of a hole in a tank.","f":[{"x":"P + (1/2)*rho*v^2 + rho*g*h = constant","n":"Bernoulli's equation along a streamline (ideal fluid, steady flow).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.51"},{"x":"v = sqrt(2*g*h)","n":"Torricelli: speed of efflux from a hole a depth h below the free surface.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.52"},{"x":"p_1 - p_2 = (1/2)*rho*(v_2^2 - v_1^2)","n":"Venturi tube: the pressure drop at the narrow part gives the flow speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 2.9, p.52"}],"w":["Faster flow means lower pressure only along the same streamline at the same height.","The efflux speed is the same as that of a body falling freely through h; it does not depend on the liquid's density."]}};
